      "problem": "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{6}=1?$",
      "worked_solution": "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(x-(-7))^{2}}{6}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=-7$ and $k=0.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-7,0).$",
